# THE CLOCK THIS FLEET'S SCHEDULES ARE WRITTEN ON.
#
# Every `schedule:` in deploy/services/*.values.yaml argues for its hour in the life of a Saudi
# workshop — «nightly», «off-hour», «when no operator has a customer standing in front of them».
# That reasoning is about Riyadh and nothing ever carried it: the chart renders a bare `schedule`,
# so k3s times every job by whatever zone the NODE happens to keep. Measured 2026-08-18, this
# fleet's node keeps Asia/Shanghai, which puts the retention sweep written for 01:17 at 20:17
# Riyadh — the busiest hour of a workshop's evening — and the weekly digest written for 09:00 at
# 04:00, a WhatsApp message to an owner before dawn. The hour was chosen with care, written down
# in prose, and read by nothing.
#
# ONE FILE, NOT ONE PER SERVICE. A zone per values file is an exceptions table wearing a different
# hat, and it invites the worse failure of half a fleet on one clock and half on another. It also
# cannot live in the values files at all: the chart's schema refuses keys it does not render, so a
# declaration there would break every deploy rather than sit quietly (which is the guard
# `check_every_values_file_renders_against_the_chart` doing its job, and how this shape was found).
#
# WHAT READS IT: ci/check_a_schedule_names_the_clock_its_hour_was_chosen_on.py, and
# deploy/verify-fleet.sh, which compares it to the zone the cluster ACTUALLY runs and says the
# drift out loud on every deploy. Neither MOVES a schedule — the chart has no `timeZone` field
# (platform/deploy/chart/templates/cronjob.yaml, read-only to us; escalated with #321). Until that
# ships this is a measured, named gap instead of an invisible one, and saying so plainly is the
# point: a check implying it had aligned the clocks would be the same defect again.
CRON_TIMEZONE=Asia/Riyadh
